 "standard_text": "Perimeter of rectangles, squares, triangles and regular polygons; area of rectangles, squares and triangles; area on a grid and rectilinear shapes (summary of NCERT Ganita Prakash Class 6)",
 "meta_desc": "Class 6 Perimeter and Area worksheet (Ganita Prakash Chapter 6): perimeter and area of rectangles, squares and triangles, composite shapes and word problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Perimeter is the distance around; area is the space inside",
 "key_sub": "Rectangle: P = 2 × (l + w), A = l × w.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>The <b>perimeter</b> is the total length of the boundary. Rectangle: 2 × (length + width). Square: 4 × side. Regular polygon: number of sides × side.</p><p>The <b>area</b> is the number of unit squares that cover a shape. Rectangle: length × width. Square: side × side. A diagonal cuts a rectangle into two triangles of half the area.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>A rectangle is 12 cm by 5 cm. Find its perimeter and area.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>P = 2 × (12 + 5)</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>A = 12 × 5</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 34 cm and 60 cm²</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>A square has perimeter 36 m. Find its area.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Side = 36 ÷ 4 = 9 m</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 81 m²</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Adding only the length and width for the perimeter (it is twice that).</li><li>Mixing up area and perimeter.</li><li>Writing the area in cm instead of cm².</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the difference between perimeter and area?",
   "a": "Perimeter is the length around a shape (in cm or m); area is the amount of surface inside it (in cm² or m²)."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do you find the perimeter of a rectangle?",
   "a": "Add all four sides: 2 × (length + width)."
  },
  {
   "q": "How is the area of a triangle related to a rectangle?",
   "a": "A diagonal cuts a rectangle into two equal triangles, so each has half the rectangle's area."
  }
 ],
